    "q-95233": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95233",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${R}^{750}$\n\nII. ${R}^{\\infty },$ the vector space of infinite real sequences\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{50}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 50$ matrices over $R$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}^{\\infty }$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of sequences is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${R}^{\\infty }$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${R}^{\\infty }$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}^{750}$ and ${\\text{M}}_{50}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{750})=750,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50}(R))={50}^{2}=2\\,500\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
